    "created_at": "2020-04-30T01:14:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-04-30T01:14:58Z",
    "body": "I second this interrogation. Seeing that lazy-loading of images didn’t work when JavaScript was disabled in the user agent, I’ve bugged people [on Mozilla’s Bugzilla](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1542784#c31) yesterday, saying that\r\n\r\n> As an author, I’ve been surprised by [the reliance on scripting being enabled]. Seeing no relationship between the unavailability of JavaScript and the loading of HTML images by the browser, I would expect `loading=\"lazy\"` to be honoured regardless of the ability to run JavaScript in the page, so that users could still benefit from the performance improvements coming from the attribute.\r\n\r\nSomeone then swiftly told this was intentional, and pointed me to the relevant part of the HTML spec.\r\n\r\nI fully understand (and agree with) the choice of favouring privacy over performance. However, I’m also wondering if we could give the user the ability to decide, via the user agent, as @SebastianZ proposes. Lazy-loading could help to save tremendous amounts of requests on image-heavy websites, so maybe we could benefit from a more nuanced solution than the current implementation, which would allow lazy-loading to still be honoured even though scripting would be disabled or unavailable.\r\n\r\n(By the way, thanks for updating the docs on MDN, Sebastian!)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
